faq:
  - q: "Where should I start with a bitcoin retirement?"
    a: "With one plan, not four tools. Open <a href='/the-bitcoin-retirement'>The Bitcoin Retirement</a>, put in a stack, a retirement year and what you want to draw, and read what comes out. Everything else here sharpens that first answer: how much is enough, which of two options to take, and whether it survives a bad market. The order on this page is the order the questions actually arrive in."
  - q: "Do I have to re-enter my numbers on each page?"
    a: "Mostly no. The tools carry your plan between them &mdash; the links each page offers put your stack, retirement year and withdrawal into the next page's inputs, so you are usually adjusting rather than retyping. Each page also writes its state into the address bar, so a link you save or share comes back to the same plan."
  - q: "Are these forecasts?"
    a: "No. Every figure across these four tools is a projection under the Power Law growth model &mdash; a description of bitcoin&rsquo;s historical trend, not a promise about its future, and its implied growth rate declines with time. <a href='/the-power-law'>The model and its limits</a> are documented on their own page. The assumptions are yours to change, which is the point of letting you change them."
  - q: "Why is the crash test a separate page?"
    a: "Because sequence-of-returns risk is a different question from plan design, and mixing them hides both. The first three tools ask what a plan does when returns arrive in trend order; the <a href='/the-bitcoin-retirement-stress-test'>Stress Test</a> asks what happens when they do not, and it varies crash depth, timing and recovery properly rather than dropping in one canned bad year."
